      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to choose the remaining 3 players out of the remaining 10 players (since Ace and Zeppo are already chosen).\nThis is a combination problem, so we need to use the combination formula:\nC(n, k) = n! / (k!(n-k)!)\nwhere C(n, k) represents the number of combinations of n items taken k at a time.\nIn this case, we have 10 players remaining to choose from, so we need to find the total number of ways to choose 3 players out of 10.\nUsing the combination formula, we get:\nC(10, 3) = 10! / (3!(10-3)!) = 10! / (3!7!) = 10 * 9 * 8 / (3 * 2 * 1) = 120\nSo, there are 120 possible ways to choose the remaining 3 players.\nHowever, we need to also consider the restriction that Ace and Zeppo must be in the starting lineup.\nThis means that the 3 players we choose must include either both Ace and Zeppo, or exactly one of them.\nThere are two cases to consider:\nCase 1: Both Ace and Zeppo are chosen\nIn this case, we only need to choose 1 more player out of the remaining 8 players (since Ace and Zeppo are already chosen).\nThe number of ways to choose 1 player out of 8 is:\nC(8, 1) = 8! / (1!(8-1)!) = 8! / (1!7!) = 8\nCase 2: Exactly one of Ace or Zeppo is chosen\nIn this case, we have 2 choices for which player to choose (Ace or Zeppo).\nThe number of ways to choose 2 players out of 10 is:\nC(10, 2) = 10! / (2!(10-2)!) = 10! / (2!8!) = 10 * 9 / (2 * 1) = 45\nSo, for each choice of Ace or Zeppo, there are 45 ways to choose the 2 other players.\nTherefore, the total number of possible starting lineups is:\nCase 1: 120 * 8 = 960\nCase 2: 2 * 45 = 90\nThe total number of possible starting lineups is:\n960 + 90 = 1050\nTherefore, there are 1050 different starting lineups possible with the given restriction.",
